add broader relation (HEAT/COLD WAVE FREQUENCY/INTENSITY [079e6699-efbf-4358-9047-b668b459fc22,39657] - EXTREME WEATHER [b29b46ad-f05f-4144-b965-5f606ce96963,39651]); 
""",
        """2024-09-23 10:32:31.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: Refers to a frequency and intensity of a heat wave and cold wave. A heat wave is a period of abnormally hot weather generally lasting more than two days. Heat waves can occur with or without high humidity. They have potential to cover a large area, exposing a high number of people to hazardous heat. A cold wave is a weather event involving a cooling of the air, or the invasion of very cold air, over a large area. It is marked by a drop of average temperature well below the averages of a region. Cold waves can have negative impacts on people, crops, properties, and services. They can be preceded or accompanied by significant winter weather events, such as blizzards or ice storms. And they can feel even colder during periods of high winds.
language code: en); 
""" ;
    skos:definition "Refers to a frequency and intensity of a heat wave and cold wave. A heat wave is a period of abnormally hot weather generally lasting more than two days. Heat waves can occur with or without high humidity. They have potential to cover a large area, exposing a high number of people to hazardous heat. A cold wave is a weather event involving a cooling of the air, or the invasion of very cold air, over a large area. It is marked by a drop of average temperature well below the averages of a region. Cold waves can have negative impacts on people, crops, properties, and services. They can be preceded or accompanied by significant winter weather events, such as blizzards or ice storms. And they can feel even colder during periods of high winds."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "HEAT/COLD WAVE FREQUENCY/INTENSITY"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "https://www.weather.gov/"@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/07a1357a-4c4f-4844-bf24-ad3290a033f4> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/b5e4912d-3224-4d7b-924c-a697677775c6> ;
    skos:definition """The Extreme Ultraviolet Imager (EUV) images the distribution of He+ in Earth's
plasmasphere by detecting its resonantly-scattered emission at 304 A. It is one
of seven scientific instruments on the IMAGE Earth satellite, an element of
NASA's MIDEX Program. EUV consists of three identical sensor heads, each having
a field of view 30 deg in diameter. These sensors are tilted relative to one
another to cover a fan-shaped field of 84x30 deg, which is swept across the
plasmasphere by the spin of the satellite.

For more information, see:
http://euv.lpl.arizona.edu/euv/"""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/instruments> ;
    skos:prefLabel "FUV"@en ;
    gcmd:altLabel [ gcmd:category "primary"@en ;
            gcmd:text "Far Ultraviolet Imager (IMAGE)"@en ] .

