    skos:changeNote """2024-10-30 12:37:43.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (GVHRR [103176cc-2353-47cf-b03e-e608e713716b,1875504] - Imaging Spectrometers/Radiometers [944b7691-af37-4fb4-9393-c114e7997829,1867017]); 
""",
        """2024-10-30 12:38:37.0 [tstevens]  
insert AltLabel (id: null
category: primary
text: Geosynchronous Very High Resolution Radiometer
language code: en); 
insert Definition (id: null
text: The Geosynchronous Very High Resolution Radiometer (GVHRR) experiment provided both day and night cloudcover data for determining cloud motions, tropical and extratropical storm life cycles, and mesoscale phenomena. They were also used for cloud climatology studies. The GVHRR had one infrared channel (10.5 to 12.5 micrometers) and one visible channel (0.55 to 0.75 micrometers). The instantaneous field of view was 3.0E-4 rad for the infrared channel (10.8-km resolution at subsatellite point) and 1.5E-4 rad for the visible channel (5.4-km resolution at subsatellite point). The dynamic range for the infrared channel was from 0 to 340 deg K and 1 to 100% albedo for the visible channel. The infrared channel had a noise equivalent temperature difference of 1.5 deg K at 200 deg K and 0.5 deg K at 300 deg K. Data from this experiment were used to determine surface temperatures and horizontal wind vectors based on cloud motions derived from sequential images formed by both channels of the GVHRR. The experiment became inoperable on August 15, 1974.
language code: en); 
""" ;
    skos:definition "The Geosynchronous Very High Resolution Radiometer (GVHRR) experiment provided both day and night cloudcover data for determining cloud motions, tropical and extratropical storm life cycles, and mesoscale phenomena. They were also used for cloud climatology studies. The GVHRR had one infrared channel (10.5 to 12.5 micrometers) and one visible channel (0.55 to 0.75 micrometers). The instantaneous field of view was 3.0E-4 rad for the infrared channel (10.8-km resolution at subsatellite point) and 1.5E-4 rad for the visible channel (5.4-km resolution at subsatellite point). The dynamic range for the infrared channel was from 0 to 340 deg K and 1 to 100% albedo for the visible channel. The infrared channel had a noise equivalent temperature difference of 1.5 deg K at 200 deg K and 0.5 deg K at 300 deg K. Data from this experiment were used to determine surface temperatures and horizontal wind vectors based on cloud motions derived from sequential images formed by both channels of the GVHRR. The experiment became inoperable on August 15, 1974."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/instruments> ;
    skos:prefLabel "GVHRR"@en ;
    gcmd:altLabel [ gcmd:category "primary"@en ;
            gcmd:text "Geosynchronous Very High Resolution Radiometer"@en ] ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/experiment/display.action?id=1974-039A-08"@en ] .

