    skos:changeNote """2012-06-25 10:42:39.0 [mpmorahan] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (MADDEN-JULIAN OSCILLATION [25d4368e-3b66-40d5-bac1-2343b127fa32,22989] - MJO [2953da71-fe1d-4506-9bd6-445b92e2b443,39709]); 
""",
        """2020-02-14 15:05:12.0 [tstevens] Remove Concepts 
delete narrower relation (null); 
""" ;
    skos:definition """In 1971 Roland Madden and Paul Julian stumbled upon a 40-50 day\r
oscillation when analyzing zonal wind anomalies in the tropical\r
Pacific. They used ten years of pressure records at Canton (at 2.8B0 S\r
in the Pacific) and upper level winds at Singapore.\r
The oscillation of surface and upper-level winds was remarkably clear\r
in Singapore. Until the early 1980's little attention was paid to this\r
oscillation, which became known as the Madden and Julian\r
Oscillation(MJO), and some scientists questioned its global\r
significance. Since the 1982-83 El Nino event, low-frequency\r
variations in the tropics, both on intra-annual (less than a year) and\r
inter-annual (more than a year) timescales, have received much more\r
attention, and the number of MJO-related publications grew rapidly."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "MADDEN-JULIAN OSCILLATION"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text """NOAA/NWS:\r
"http://ww2.wrh.noaa.gov/climate_info/Madden-Julian_Oscillation.htm"\r
For a full bibliography of MJO papers, see:\r
"http://www.coaps.fsu.edu/lib/elninobib/Madden-Julian-Oscillation/">"""@en ] .

