        """2019-03-26 14:42:29.0 [sritz]  
insert AltLabel (id: null
category: primary
text: Bare neutron monitor (lead-free)
language code: en); 
""",
        """2024-12-19 12:28:07.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: Neutron monitors of standard design are employed worldwide to study variations in the ﬂux of galactic cosmic rays and solar energetic particles in the GeV range. The design minimizes detector response to neutrons below ∼10 MeV produced by cosmic ray interactions in the ambient medium. Increasingly, however, such neutrons are of interest as a means of obtaining spectral information on cosmic rays, for studies of soil moisture, and for nuclear threat detection. Bare neutron counters, a type of lead-free neutron monitor, can detect such neutrons, but comparatively little work has been done to characterize the dependence of their count rate on cutoﬀ rigidity.
language code: en); 
""" ;
    skos:definition "Neutron monitors of standard design are employed worldwide to study variations in the ﬂux of galactic cosmic rays and solar energetic particles in the GeV range. The design minimizes detector response to neutrons below ∼10 MeV produced by cosmic ray interactions in the ambient medium. Increasingly, however, such neutrons are of interest as a means of obtaining spectral information on cosmic rays, for studies of soil moisture, and for nuclear threat detection. Bare neutron counters, a type of lead-free neutron monitor, can detect such neutrons, but comparatively little work has been done to characterize the dependence of their count rate on cutoﬀ rigidity."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/instruments> ;
    skos:prefLabel "NM BARE"@en ;
    gcmd:altLabel [ gcmd:category "primary"@en ;
            gcmd:text "Bare neutron monitor (lead-free)"@en ] ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "https://pos.sissa.it/358/114/"@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/27dd85c2-3403-438d-8b0c-8d424df60468> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/112e71ec-c0a1-49a8-82d7-bcb317b45860> ;
    skos:changeNote """2012-06-26 12:13:07.0 [gee-cee] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (SOIL MOISTURE [27dd85c2-3403-438d-8b0c-8d424df60468,39861] - LAND SURFACE/AGRICULTURE INDICATORS [112e71ec-c0a1-49a8-82d7-bcb317b45860,39835]); 
""",
        """2012-07-18 18:19:54.0 [mpmorahan]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: Soil moisture is difficult to define because it means different things in different disciplines. For example, a farmer's concept of soil moisture is different from that of a water resource manager or a weather forecaster. Generally, however, soil moisture is the water that is held in the spaces between soil particles. Surface soil moisture is the water that is in the upper 10 cm of soil, whereas root zone soil moisture is the water that is available to plants, which is generally considered to be in the upper 200 cm of soil.
language code: en); 
""" ;
    skos:definition "Soil moisture is difficult to define because it means different things in different disciplines. For example, a farmer's concept of soil moisture is different from that of a water resource manager or a weather forecaster. Generally, however, soil moisture is the water that is held in the spaces between soil particles. Surface soil moisture is the water that is in the upper 10 cm of soil, whereas root zone soil moisture is the water that is available to plants, which is generally considered to be in the upper 200 cm of soil."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "SOIL MOISTURE"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "Marshall Space Flight Center Earth Science Office: http://wwwghcc.msfc.nasa.gov/landprocess/lp_home.html"@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/27f6b649-91b4-40ac-b505-29ee0fe73ee9> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/ecd58f82-8f96-45e1-9547-dd4a02291bd9> ;
    skos:changeNote """2021-12-14 08:57:45.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (HOxCIMS [27f6b649-91b4-40ac-b505-29ee0fe73ee9,835985] - Spectrometers/Radiometers [ecd58f82-8f96-45e1-9547-dd4a02291bd9,828525]); 
""" ;
    skos:definition "An inlet collects ambient air from the free air stream and adds reagents, including O2 or N2 dilutents, and NO and SO2 reagent gases. This method, called \"oxygen dilution modulation\" leads to nearly 100% measurement of HO2 and RO2 in the O2 dilution/low reagent concentration mode, whereas RO2 is measured with less than 10% efficiency in the N2 dilution/higher reagent concentration mode. This is because the chemistry converts peroxy radicals to H2SO4 efficiently in the O2 mode, but RO2 radicals are converted to RONO in the N2 mode. The H2SO4 thus produced is ionized by reaction with NO3- ions. The reagent and product ions are detected by mass spectrometry using quadrupole mass filtering and counting by a channel electron multiplier operating in the negative ion mode."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/instruments> ;
    skos:prefLabel "HOxCIMS"@en ;
    gcmd:altLabel [ gcmd:category "primary"@en ;
            gcmd:text "HOx Chemical Ionization Mass Spectrometer"@en ] ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "https://airbornescience.nasa.gov/instrument/HOxCIMS"@en ] .

