<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/2ddd003d-c19f-4336-9837-316cce5efe0b> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/50b8fe04-9149-4b7f-a8b2-b33b1e3aa192> ;
    skos:definition """Albedo is the ratio of the radiation (radiant energy or luminous
energy) reflected by a surface to that incident on it. Snow and cloud
surfaces have a high albedo, because most of the energy of the visible
solar spectrum is reflected. Vegetation and ocean surfaces have low
albedo, because they absorb a large fraction of the energy. Clouds are the
chief cause of variations in the Earth's albedo."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "ALBEDO"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text """Maunder, W.J. 1994. Dictionary of Global Climate Change, 2nd ed. Chapman and
Hall, NY. WMO, 1966. International Meteorological Vocabulary, WMO/OMM/BMO - No.
182 TP. 91, Switzerland."""@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/2de06b90-4abe-4c71-a537-978679bf8aea> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/b887d3e5-4280-43d2-a34e-0f63ac086b6a> ;
    skos:changeNote """2012-06-25 11:38:07.0 [mpmorahan] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (PACIFIC DECADAL OSCILLATION [2de06b90-4abe-4c71-a537-978679bf8aea,22991] - PDO [8a31313b-e60e-47e8-b7bd-df0503e7c868,39719]); 
""",
        """2020-02-14 15:39:02.0 [tstevens] Remove Concepts 
delete narrower relation (null); 
""" ;
    skos:definition """Fisheries scientist Steven Hare coined the term "Pacific Decadal\r
Oscillation" (PDO) in 1996 while researching connections between\r
Alaska salmon production cycles and Pacific climate. PDO has since\r
been described as a long-lived El Nino-like pattern of Pacific\r
climate variability because the two climate oscillations have similar\r
spatial climate fingerprints, but very different temporal behavior.\r
Two main characteristics distinguish PDO from El Nino/ Southern\r
Oscillation (ENSO): first, 20th century PDO "events" persisted for\r
20-to-30 years, while typical ENSO events persisted for 6 to 18\r
months; second, the climatic fingerprints of the PDO are most visible\r
in the North Pacific/North American sector, while secondary signatures\r
exist in the tropics - the opposite is true for ENSO.\r
Several independent studies find evidence for just two full PDO cycles\r
in the past century: "cool" PDO regimes prevailed from 1890-1924 and\r
again from 1947-1976 while"warm" PDO regimes dominated from 1925-1946\r
and from 1977 through (at least) the mid-1990's. Shoshiro Minobe; has\r
shown that 20th century PDO fluctuations were most energetic in two\r
general periodicities, one from 15-to-25 years, and the other from\r
50-to-70 years."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "PACIFIC DECADAL OSCILLATION"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text """Hare, S.R. and R.C. Francis. 1995. Climate Change and Salmon\r
Production in the Northeast Pacific Ocean. In: R.J.  Beamish [ed.]\r
Ocean climate and northern fish populations. Can. spec. Pub. Fish. Aquat. Sci.\r
121, pp. 357-372.\r
"http://ww2.wrh.noaa.gov/climate_info/PDO_page.htm\""""@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/2de882f0-d84a-471e-8fb5-9f8a1c7913c1> a skos:Concept ;
