<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/31f77d6b-72f7-45e6-93be-8ac5fd5dc373> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/ae35f430-6534-49de-8b4c-edfc1e98870a> ;
    skos:changeNote """2013-02-18 09:54:12.0 [tbs1979] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (AE INDEX [31f77d6b-72f7-45e6-93be-8ac5fd5dc373,82411] - GEOMAGNETIC INDICES [ae35f430-6534-49de-8b4c-edfc1e98870a,62733]); 
""",
        """2013-03-11 14:57:16.0 [tbs1979]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: The AE Index is designed to provide a global, quantitative measure of auroral zone magnetic activity produced by enhanced Ionospheric currents flowing below and within the auroral oval. Ideally, It is the total range of deviation at an instant of time from quiet day values of the horizontal magnetic field (h) around the auroral oval. Defined and developed by Davis and Sugiura [1966], AE has been usefully employed both qualitatively and quantitatively as a correlative index in studies of substorm morphology, the behavior of communication satellites, radio propagation, radio scintillation, and the coupling between the interplanetary magnetic field and the earth's magnetosphere. For these varied uses, AE possesses advantages over other geomagnetic indices or at least shares their advantageous properties.
language code: en); 
""" ;
    skos:definition "The AE Index is designed to provide a global, quantitative measure of auroral zone magnetic activity produced by enhanced Ionospheric currents flowing below and within the auroral oval. Ideally, It is the total range of deviation at an instant of time from quiet day values of the horizontal magnetic field (h) around the auroral oval. Defined and developed by Davis and Sugiura [1966], AE has been usefully employed both qualitatively and quantitatively as a correlative index in studies of substorm morphology, the behavior of communication satellites, radio propagation, radio scintillation, and the coupling between the interplanetary magnetic field and the earth's magnetosphere. For these varied uses, AE possesses advantages over other geomagnetic indices or at least shares their advantageous properties."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "AE INDEX"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "NOAA"@en ] .

