by the 2.4 and 2.3 micrometer channels, respectively, sense solar radiation reflected from the surface. The MOPITT is a scanning instrument with a field-of-view of 1.8 degrees (22-km footprint at nadir). The scan line consists of 28 pixels, each at 1.8 degree increments with a maximum scan angle of 25.2 degrees off-axis (swath width of 620-km). MOPITT data products are expected to include gridded retrievals of methane with a horizontal resolution of 120 km at 1 % accuracy and gridded CO soundings with 10 % accuracy in three vertical layers between 0 and 15 km.  The MOPITT instrument was launched on the NASA Earth  Observing System (EOS) Terra spacecraft on December 18, 1999.  

