<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/3da6855e-9be8-4a79-826e-4ce984ed49a5> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/de62c07e-96c6-44fb-a3a1-cd2902305691> ;
    skos:changeNote """2019-05-30 08:26:25.0 [mmorahan] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (Cryospheric Feedbacks [3da6855e-9be8-4a79-826e-4ce984ed49a5,368863] - Climate Feedbacks [de62c07e-96c6-44fb-a3a1-cd2902305691,368851]); 
""",
        """2019-06-03 14:45:53.0 [mmorahan] Rename Concept 
update PrefLabel (CRYOSPHERIC FEEDBACKS); 
""" ;
    skos:definition """Processes within the cryosphere through which the climate system is
controlled, changed, or modulated. Cryospheric feedbacks involve a
perturbation in a climate quantity that causes a change in a second
quantity, and the change in the second quantity ultimately leads to an
additional change in the first. Cryospheric feedbacks may, for example,
involve the dependence of surface albedo on the presence of ice and
snow."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "CRYOSPHERIC FEEDBACKS"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "Adapted from the IPCC TAR (https://www.ipcc.ch/site/assets/uploads/2018/03/TAR-07.pdf) and the USGCRP Glossary (https://www.globalchange.gov/climate-change/glossary)"@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/3dd67068-9e9f-4a5c-ae4b-799fbb0332ec> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/5b753e40-b3f1-426a-8d92-ffee1d675468> ;
    skos:definition """The Airborne Multichannel Microwave Radiometer (AMMR) measures
thermal microwave emission (in degrees Kelvin of brightness
temperature) from surface and atmosphere. The up-looking
radiometer at 21 and 37 GHz is a component of AMMR that was
developed in the 1970's for precipitation measurements from an
aircraft. The entire AMMR assembly covers a frequency range of
10-92 GHz. The 21/37 GHz unit has been flown in many types of
aircraft during the past three decades in various field
campaigns. It was refurbished during the year 2000 and is ready
for flights again.

Additional information available at
"http://nsidc.org/data/amsr_validation/rainfall/wakasa_bay/AMMR.html"

[Summary provided by NSIDC]"""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/instruments> ;
    skos:prefLabel "AMMR"@en ;
