""" ;
    skos:definition """The SPI was formulated by Tom Mckee, Nolan Doesken and John Kleist of\r
the Colorado Climate Center in 1993. The purpose is to assign a single\r
numeric value to the precipitation which can be compared across\r
regions with markedly different climates. Technically, the SPI is the\r
number of standard deviations that the observed value would deviate\r
from the long-term mean, for a normally distributed random\r
variable. Since precipitation is not normally distributed, a\r
transformation is first applied so that the transformed precipitation\r
values follow a normal distribution.\r
The Standardized Precipitation Index was designed to explicitly\r
express the fact that it is possible to simultaneously experience wet\r
conditions on one or more time scales, and dry conditions at other\r
time scales, often a difficult concept to convey in simple terms to\r
decision-makers. Consequently, a separate SPI value is calculated for\r
a selection of time scales, covering the last 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8,\r
9, 10, 11, 12, 15, 18, 24, 30, 36, 48, 60, and 72 months, and ending\r
on the last day of the latest month."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "STANDARDIZED PRECIPITATION INDEX"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text """McKee, T.B., N.J. Doesken, and J. Kliest. 1993: The relationship of drought\r
frequency and duration to time scales. Preprints, <i>Eighth Conference on\r
Applied Climatology</i>, Anaheim, CA, American Meterological Society, 179-184.\r
<P>\r
See also the Desert Research Institute (DRI)\r
"http://www.wrcc.dri.edu/spi/spi.html\""""@en ] .

