    skos:changeNote """2013-02-14 09:41:38.0 [mpmorahan] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (PYROCUMULONIMBUS [772d8044-f11b-4b01-bc72-d7dd45cfe1b3,82213] - CUMULONIMBUS [7c4d5f8f-4809-4859-b379-3b8c379bc83c,82205]); 
""",
        """2013-03-20 19:01:07.0 [mpmorahan]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: Pyrocumulonimbus is the fire-breathing dragon of clouds. A cumulonimbus without the ""pyre"" part is imposing enough -- a massive, anvil-shaped tower of power reaching five miles (8 km) high, hurling thunderbolts, wind and rain.

Add smoke and fire to the mix and you have pyrocumulonimbus, an explosive storm cloud actually created by the smoke and heat from fire, and which can ravage tens of thousands of acres. And in the process, ""pyroCb"" storms funnel their smoke like a chimney into Earth's stratosphere, with lingering ill effects.
language code: en); 
""" ;
    skos:definition """Pyrocumulonimbus is the fire-breathing dragon of clouds. A cumulonimbus without the ""pyre"" part is imposing enough -- a massive, anvil-shaped tower of power reaching five miles (8 km) high, hurling thunderbolts, wind and rain.

Add smoke and fire to the mix and you have pyrocumulonimbus, an explosive storm cloud actually created by the smoke and heat from fire, and which can ravage tens of thousands of acres. And in the process, ""pyroCb"" storms funnel their smoke like a chimney into Earth's stratosphere, with lingering ill effects."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "PYROCUMULONIMBUS"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "NASA http://www.nasa.gov/topics/earth/features/pyrocb.html\""@en ] .

