<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/835fb659-6a25-44a7-8c5a-7698fe785472> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/944b7691-af37-4fb4-9393-c114e7997829> ;
    skos:definition """The Advanced Visible and Near Infrared Radiometer type 2 (AVNIR-2) is a visible and near infrared radiometer for observing land and coastal zones and provides better spatial land coverage maps and land-use classification maps for monitoring regional environment. The AVNIR-2 is a successor to the AVNIR onboard the Advanced Earth Observing Satellite (ADEOS) launched in August 1996.

Its main improvement over AVNIR's is its instantaneous field-of-view (IFOV). The AVNIR-2 provides 10-meter spatial resolution images compared with the 16 m resolution of the AVNIR in the multi spectral region. The higher resolution was realized by improving the CCD detectors (AVNIR: 5,000 pixels per CCD, AVNIR-2: 7,000 pixels per CCD) and their electronics. Another improvement is a cross track pointing function for prompt observation of disaster areas. The pointing angle of AVNIR-2 is + and - 44 degrees.

[Summary Provided by JAXA.]


