<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/a38db528-3064-449d-ae70-af86997a11f4> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/f7e97dc3-1181-41b5-8b90-946eb2504110> ;
    skos:changeNote """2018-03-07 08:30:05.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (SALINE CONCENTRATION [a38db528-3064-449d-ae70-af86997a11f4,310687] - WATER CHARACTERISTICS [f7e97dc3-1181-41b5-8b90-946eb2504110,310633]); 
""",
        """2018-03-07 12:35:26.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: The concentration of dissolved salts in water, usually expressed in parts per thousand (permille, %) or parts per million (ppm). The United States Geological Survey classifies saline water in three salinity categories. Salt concentration in slightly saline water is around 1,000 to 3,000 ppm (0.1–0.3%), in moderately saline water 3,000 to 10,000 ppm (0.3–1%) and in highly saline water 10,000 to 35,000 ppm (1–3.5%). Seawater has a salinity of roughly 35,000 ppm, equivalent to 35 grams of salt per one liter (or kilogram) of water.
language code: en); 
""",
        """2018-03-14 09:30:23.0 [tstevens]  
update Definition (Fundamentals of Chemistry); 
""" ;
    skos:definition "The concentration of dissolved salts in water, usually expressed in parts per thousand (permille, %) or parts per million (ppm). The United States Geological Survey classifies saline water in three salinity categories. Salt concentration in slightly saline water is around 1,000 to 3,000 ppm (0.1–0.3%), in moderately saline water 3,000 to 10,000 ppm (0.3–1%) and in highly saline water 10,000 to 35,000 ppm (1–3.5%). Seawater has a salinity of roughly 35,000 ppm, equivalent to 35 grams of salt per one liter (or kilogram) of water."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "SALINE CONCENTRATION"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "Fundamentals of Chemistry"@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/a394776f-b658-44de-b952-90f4e53d58cc> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/a9394dee-8c4e-47a8-b630-6549ac1cb717> ;
    skos:definition """Drought is a protracted period of deficient precipitation resulting in\r
extensive damage to crops, resulting in loss of yield.\r
Lack of rainfall for an extended period of time can bring farmers and major\r
metropolitan areas to their knees. It does not take very long; a few rain-free\r
weeks spreads panic and shrivels crops. A drought forecast would be issued for
one of the following:<BR><BR>\r
Meteorological - a measure of departure of precipitation from normal.  Due to\r
climatic differences what is considered a drought in one location may not be a\r
drought in another location.<p>\r
Agricultural - refers to a situation when the amount of moisture in the soil no
longer meets the needs of a particular crop and can also affect livestock and\r
other dry-land agricultural operations.<P>\r
Hydrological -	occurs when surface and subsurface water supplies are below\r
normal.<p>\r
Socioeconomic - refers to the situation that occurs when physical water\r
shortage begins to affect people.<p>"""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "DROUGHT FORECAST"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text """http://drought.unl.edu/<br>\r
http://www.droughtoutlook.com/"""@en ] .

