<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/a759efc0-0eb4-4e41-84f3-c6f49cca12ce> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/055a79c7-61db-4250-abad-f1e09909f14c> ;
    skos:definition """[Source: National Space Science Data Center, http://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/experimentDisplay.do?id=1972-089A-03 ]

Special Sensor J (SSJ) was an electron spectrograph with one fixed cahnnel and one stepping channel. The channels detected energetic electrons over ranges of energies associated with visible aurora. The fixed channel was 6 keV and the stepping channel cycled through eight energy thresholds: 54, 98, 219, 600, 1400, 3540, 8200, and 1970 eV. The data sample was taken approximately every second. The field-of-view was 3 degrees by 12 degrees.


