    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/4ad0c52d-6449-48ff-8678-adc6b2cebcb7> ;
    skos:changeNote """2016-08-29 13:06:28.0 [mpmorahan] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (POLARIZED REFLECTANCE [a87d6473-3a03-4bc6-aa21-6157fae96b8e,277829] - ATMOSPHERIC RADIATION [4ad0c52d-6449-48ff-8678-adc6b2cebcb7,83505]); 
""" ;
    skos:definition "To cause the electrical and magnetic fields associated with electromagnetic waves, especially light, to vibrate in a particular direction or path. The transverse electric and magnetic waves always vibrate at right angles to each other, but in ordinary unpolarized light sources, the direction of polarization of each wave is randomly distributed. Light can be polarized by reflection, and by passing through certain materials."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "POLARIZED REFLECTANCE"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "The American Heritage® Science Dictionary"@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/a8952068-667a-4d77-8e4c-e40bcd310cdd> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/deeecd30-32e0-4b89-ae24-31e3e6641b4c> ;
    skos:definition """The Geostationary Meteorological Satellites (GMS) were Japan&#039;s contribution to the international Global Atmospheric Research Program (GARP).  The GMS series carried the Visible and Spin Scan Radiometer (VISSR).  The satellite was spin-stabilized with a despun earth-pointing antenna.  The satellite was positioned near 140 deg E and was designed to operate for 5 years.  This was a follow-on GMS type spacecraft launched and controlled by NASDA of Japan. The spacecraft was launched in August 1984, and turned off in December 1989.
