<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/a8d3f9a0-be0b-4690-86b9-ac64d951886a> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/c7b5c02c-724d-4a19-b824-98180f3900c9> ;
    skos:definition """Forest Composition refers to the tree species which make up the forest.\r
Vegetation Structure refers to the vertical arrangement of canopy layers and\r
plants of different life forms, or the horizontal variation in canopy\r
closure and canopy layers, or both.  Vegetation Structure also includes\r
standing dead trees (snags) and decaying logs on the ground (coarse woody\r
debris)."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "FOREST COMPOSITION/VEGETATION STRUCTURE"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text """Kimmins, J.P. 1997. Forest Ecology.  Prentice Hall.  Upper Saddle River, New\r
Jersey."""@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/a8f6e91f-7875-4597-8ab8-64f4b14e8b49> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/13b8bcd4-7566-49e5-9975-ea15470474ab> ;
    skos:definition """A general term covering a wide variety of mass-movement landforms and processes
involving the downslope transport, under gravitational influence, of soil and\r
rock material en masse.  Usually the displaced material move over a relatively\r
confined zone or surface of shear.  The wide range of sites and structures, and
of material properties affecting resistance to shear, result in a great range\r
of landslide morephology, rates, patterns of movement, and scale.  Landsliding\r
is usually preceded, accompanied, and followed by imperceptible creep along the
surface of sliding and/or within the slide mass.  Terminology designating\r
landslide types generally refers to the landform as well as the process\r
responsible for it, e.g. rockfall, translational slide, block glide, avalanche,
mudflow, liquefaction slide and slump. <br><br> \r
A Landslide Advisory may be included in other types of advisories such as ones\r
for earthquakes, volcanic activity, and floods. Landslide advisories may also
take the form of landslide susceptibility and probability maps."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "LANDSLIDES"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text """Jackson, Julia A. (1997) <i>Glossary of Geology, 4th Edition</i>.  Alexandria,\r
VA: American Geological Institute, 769 p.<p>\r
See "http://www-socal.wr.usgs.gov/north/landslide.html" as an example.<br>\r
For contributing factors to landslides,see
"http://landslides.usgs.gov/html_files/nlic/page5.html"."""@en ] .

