""" ;
    skos:definition """The North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO) index is often defined as the\r
difference of sea-level pressure between 2 stations situated close to\r
the "centres of action" over Iceland and the Azores. Stykkisholmur\r
(Iceland) is invariably used as the northern station, whereas either;\r
Ponta Delgada (Azores) Lisbon (Portugal) Gibraltar are used as the\r
southern station.\r
Strong positive phases of the NAO tend to be associated with\r
above-normal temperatures in the eastern United States and across\r
northern Europe and below-normal temperatures in Greenland and\r
oftentimes across southern Europe and the Middle East. They are also\r
associated with above-normal precipitation over northern Europe and\r
Scandinavia and below-normal precipitation over southern and central\r
Europe. Opposite patterns of temperature and precipitation anomalies\r
are typically observed during strong negative phases of the\r
NAO. During particularly prolonged periods dominated by one particular\r
phase of the NAO, abnormal height and temperature patterns are also\r
often seen extending well into central Russia and north-central\r
Siberia."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:prefLabel "NORTH ATLANTIC OSCILLATION"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text """From NOAA/NWS:\r
http://ww2.wrh.noaa.gov/climate_info/Arctic_N_Atlantic_Oscillation.htm"\r
See also: "http://horizon.atmos.colostate.edu/ao/"""@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/c6015706-d663-4d06-ae76-c25af7fa83fe> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/5f5e4f2c-61b7-4694-8680-3fc5799f6171> ;
    skos:definition """The Ion Composition Instrument (ICI) on EQUATOR-S measures the 3D distribution
functions of the major ion species, H+, He+, He++, and O+. It consists of a
retarding-potential analyzer (RPA), followed by a toroidal energy-per-charge
(E/q) analyzer with disc-shaped field of view, followed by a 20 kV
post-acceleration into a time-of-flight (ToF) analysis section. Without RPA
operation, the E/q range is 15 V to 40 kV, otherwise it starts at essentially
zero volts. To accommodate the large dynamic range in ion fluxes, the
instrument has split the 360¡ FoV into two 180¡ sections whose sensitivities
differ by a factor 100. Moments of the distributions of all 4 ion species are
computed on board and are available every 4 spins, i.e., every 6 s. The
instrument is identical to the CODIF portion of the CIS instrument on CLUSTER.
See:
http://www.mpe-garching.mpg.de/EQS/eq-s-instruments.html"""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/instruments> ;
    skos:prefLabel "ICI"@en ;
    gcmd:altLabel [ gcmd:category "primary"@en ;
            gcmd:text "Ion Composition Instrument"@en ] .

