<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/c6962bbc-9332-48fb-96d7-0e2cb14fb4d9> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/47d0c7c0-683c-42a9-b53b-53b684972b52> ;
    skos:changeNote """2025-03-07 10:39:45.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: A device that combines the capabilities of both a GNSS receiver and an IMU. These systems provide highly accurate and reliable navigation and positioning data, and are widely used in a variety of applications, such as aviation, maritime navigation, and autonomous vehicles. A GNSS receiver is a device that receives signals from satellites orbiting the Earth and uses the information contained in these signals to determine the user’s position and velocity. An IMU, on the other hand, is a device that measures the user’s acceleration and angular velocity. When a GNSS IMU system is in operation, it receives signals from multiple GNSS satellites and uses this information to determine the user’s position and velocity. At the same time, it also measures the user’s acceleration and angular velocity using an IMU. This data is then combined to provide a highly accurate and reliable estimate of the user’s position, velocity, and orientation.
language code: en); 
""" ;
    skos:definition "A device that combines the capabilities of both a GNSS receiver and an IMU. These systems provide highly accurate and reliable navigation and positioning data, and are widely used in a variety of applications, such as aviation, maritime navigation, and autonomous vehicles. A GNSS receiver is a device that receives signals from satellites orbiting the Earth and uses the information contained in these signals to determine the user’s position and velocity. An IMU, on the other hand, is a device that measures the user’s acceleration and angular velocity. When a GNSS IMU system is in operation, it receives signals from multiple GNSS satellites and uses this information to determine the user’s position and velocity. At the same time, it also measures the user’s acceleration and angular velocity using an IMU. This data is then combined to provide a highly accurate and reliable estimate of the user’s position, velocity, and orientation."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/instruments> ;
    skos:prefLabel "GPS/IMU"@en ;
    gcmd:altLabel [ gcmd:category "primary"@en ;
            gcmd:text "Global Positioning System/Inertial Measurement Unit"@en ] ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "https://globalgpssystems.com/gnss/introduction-to-gnss-imu-systems/"@en ] .

