<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/ddd0f410-c84f-4c72-b6ed-7efbcbf3c244> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/5b753e40-b3f1-426a-8d92-ffee1d675468> ;
    skos:changeNote """2018-04-19 08:51:28.0 [mmorahan] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (AMR-2 [ddd0f410-c84f-4c72-b6ed-7efbcbf3c244,367543] - Radiometers [5b753e40-b3f1-426a-8d92-ffee1d675468,347283]); 
""",
        """2018-04-19 09:05:03.0 [mmorahan]  
insert AltLabel (id: null
category: primary
text: Advanced Microwave Radiometer-2
language code: en); 
insert Definition (id: null
text: The AMR-2 instrument is provided by NASA/JPL with the objective to measure the altimeter signal path delay due to tropospheric water vapor. AMR-2 is a passive microwave radiometer measuring the brightness temperatures in the nadir column at 18.7, 23.8, and 34 GHz, providing path delay correction for the altimeter (the brightness temperatures are converted to path-delay information). The 23.8 GHz channel is the primary water vapor sensor, the 34 GHz channel provides a correction for non-raining clouds, and the 18.7 GHz channel provides the correction for effects of wind-induced enhancements in the sea surface background emission.

The AMR-2 consists of two subsystems: ESA (Electronics Structure Assembly) and RSA (Reflector Structure Assembly). The ESA is developed by JPL, while the RSA is developed by ATK Space Systems, San Diego, CA.
language code: en); 
""" ;
    skos:definition """The AMR-2 instrument is provided by NASA/JPL with the objective to measure the altimeter signal path delay due to tropospheric water vapor. AMR-2 is a passive microwave radiometer measuring the brightness temperatures in the nadir column at 18.7, 23.8, and 34 GHz, providing path delay correction for the altimeter (the brightness temperatures are converted to path-delay information). The 23.8 GHz channel is the primary water vapor sensor, the 34 GHz channel provides a correction for non-raining clouds, and the 18.7 GHz channel provides the correction for effects of wind-induced enhancements in the sea surface background emission.

The AMR-2 consists of two subsystems: ESA (Electronics Structure Assembly) and RSA (Reflector Structure Assembly). The ESA is developed by JPL, while the RSA is developed by ATK Space Systems, San Diego, CA."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/instruments> ;
    skos:prefLabel "AMR-2"@en ;
    gcmd:altLabel [ gcmd:category "primary"@en ;
            gcmd:text "Advanced Microwave Radiometer-2"@en ] ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "https://earth.esa.int/web/eoportal/satellite-missions/j/jason-3"@en ] .

