<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/ba49c933-5787-4e1f-a408-bc4af632e00a> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/ecd58f82-8f96-45e1-9547-dd4a02291bd9> ;
    skos:changeNote """2020-02-28 10:56:27.0 [sritz] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (Hyperspectral Spectrometers/Radiometers [ba49c933-5787-4e1f-a408-bc4af632e00a,559839] - Spectrometers/Radiometers [ecd58f82-8f96-45e1-9547-dd4a02291bd9,546159]); 
""",
        """2020-02-28 10:57:22.0 [sritz] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (Hyperspectral Spectrometers/Radiometers [ba49c933-5787-4e1f-a408-bc4af632e00a,559839] - Field Spectroradiometer [618d5de2-7915-4710-b1a2-09ed87a1debc,559843]); 
""",
        """2024-12-06 11:41:57.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: While humans can only observe red, green, and blue color spectrums, hyperspectral spectrophotometers measure a material’s continuous spectrum of light with the power of fine wavelength resolution. This resolution captures visible light spectrums as well as those that are near-infrared. Hyperspectral spectrophotometers display this data in a hyperspectral cube format where two dimensions represent the spatial extent of the material’s composition and the third represents its spectral content. Compared to other spectrophotometers, hyperspectral spectrophotometry observes hundreds to thousands more spectral bands and provides a narrower spectral resolution that’s only a few nanometers wide. These tools are useful for determining the wavelength resolutions of both solids and liquids in rapid time without waiting for the aid of processing systems.
language code: en); 
""" ;
    skos:definition "While humans can only observe red, green, and blue color spectrums, hyperspectral spectrophotometers measure a material’s continuous spectrum of light with the power of fine wavelength resolution. This resolution captures visible light spectrums as well as those that are near-infrared. Hyperspectral spectrophotometers display this data in a hyperspectral cube format where two dimensions represent the spatial extent of the material’s composition and the third represents its spectral content. Compared to other spectrophotometers, hyperspectral spectrophotometry observes hundreds to thousands more spectral bands and provides a narrower spectral resolution that’s only a few nanometers wide. These tools are useful for determining the wavelength resolutions of both solids and liquids in rapid time without waiting for the aid of processing systems."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/instruments> ;
    skos:narrower <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/618d5de2-7915-4710-b1a2-09ed87a1debc> ;
    skos:prefLabel "Hyperspectral Spectrometers/Radiometers"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "https://www.hunterlab.com/blog/what-is-a-hyperspectral-spectrophotometer/"@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/ba4a9964-8323-45df-a372-b4e2f3eef9e5> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/62019831-aaba-4d63-a5cd-73138ccfa5d0> ;
    skos:changeNote """2013-02-14 12:46:29.0 [mpmorahan] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (VORTEX STREET [ba4a9964-8323-45df-a372-b4e2f3eef9e5,82295] - CLOUD DYNAMICS [62019831-aaba-4d63-a5cd-73138ccfa5d0,82131]); 
""",
        """2013-02-14 12:48:23.0 [mpmorahan] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (VORTEX STREET [ba4a9964-8323-45df-a372-b4e2f3eef9e5,82295] - KARMAN VORTEX STREET [2d00d3c4-2ef3-49f6-9261-6184f6517b4f,82299]); 
""",
        """2013-03-21 14:17:41.0 [mpmorahan]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: (Also called Kármán vortex street, vortex trail, vortex train.) Two parallel rows of alternately placed vortices along the wake of an obstacle in a fluid of moderate Reynolds number.

Fluid drag can be calculated from the motion of these vortices, which are stable only for a certain ratio of the width of the street to the distance between vortices along the street.
language code: en); 
""" ;
    skos:definition """(Also called Kármán vortex street, vortex trail, vortex train.) Two parallel rows of alternately placed vortices along the wake of an obstacle in a fluid of moderate Reynolds number.

Fluid drag can be calculated from the motion of these vortices, which are stable only for a certain ratio of the width of the street to the distance between vortices along the street."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:narrower <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/2d00d3c4-2ef3-49f6-9261-6184f6517b4f> ;
    skos:prefLabel "VORTEX STREET"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "American Meteorological Society (http://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Main_Page)"@en ] .

<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/bad22a08-f8ab-49b3-b266-005b21496626> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/1506fb17-7ac4-44ce-bde5-074885bdb2d2> ;
    skos:changeNote """2017-08-14 15:23:49.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add broader relation (IRNSS (India’s Regional Navigation Satellite System) [bad22a08-f8ab-49b3-b266-005b21496626,309919] - Navigation Platforms [1506fb17-7ac4-44ce-bde5-074885bdb2d2,287403]); 
""",
        """2017-08-14 15:23:59.0 [tstevens] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (IRNSS (India’s Regional Navigation Satellite System) [bad22a08-f8ab-49b3-b266-005b21496626,309919] - IRNSS [4c93cc0b-ca0e-4421-ac60-559b6390b89b,309923]); 
""",
        """2017-08-15 07:34:37.0 [tstevens]  
update PrefLabel (IRNSS (India's Regional Navigation Satellite System)); 
""",
        """2017-08-15 09:31:18.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: IRNSS is an independent regional navigation satellite system being developed by India. It is designed to provide accurate position information service to users in India as well as the region extending up to 1500 km from its boundary, which is its primary service area. An Extended Service Area lies between primary service area and area enclosed by the rectangle from Latitude 30 deg South to 50 deg North, Longitude 30 deg East to 130 deg East.

IRNSS will provide two types of services, namely, Standard Positioning Service (SPS) which is provided to all the users and Restricted Service (RS), which is an encrypted service provided only to the authorised users. The IRNSS System is expected to provide a position accuracy of better than 20 m in the primary service area.
language code: en); 
""",
        """2021-12-01 12:40:40.0 [tstevens]  
update PrefLabel (India's Regional Navigation Satellite System (IRNSS)); 
""" ;
    skos:definition """IRNSS is an independent regional navigation satellite system being developed by India. It is designed to provide accurate position information service to users in India as well as the region extending up to 1500 km from its boundary, which is its primary service area. An Extended Service Area lies between primary service area and area enclosed by the rectangle from Latitude 30 deg South to 50 deg North, Longitude 30 deg East to 130 deg East.

IRNSS will provide two types of services, namely, Standard Positioning Service (SPS) which is provided to all the users and Restricted Service (RS), which is an encrypted service provided only to the authorised users. The IRNSS System is expected to provide a position accuracy of better than 20 m in the primary service area."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/platforms> ;
    skos:narrower <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/4c93cc0b-ca0e-4421-ac60-559b6390b89b> ;
    skos:prefLabel "India's Regional Navigation Satellite System (IRNSS)"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text "http://www.isro.gov.in/irnss-programme"@en ] .

