<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/c82f3480-545f-4491-83f1-0477369ddcd8> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/376a1d5c-2496-4381-981f-bc047af92044> ;
    skos:changeNote """2015-12-10 17:11:34.0 [aaleman] Insert Concept 
add narrower relation (PERMAFROST [c82f3480-545f-4491-83f1-0477369ddcd8,84051] - PERMAFROST TEMPERATURE [d8606e80-3d34-4540-a355-5f99737f7ab7,158553]); 
""" ;
    skos:definition """1. (Also called perennially frozen ground, pergelisol, permanently frozen\r
ground.) A layer of soil or bedrock at a variable depth beneath the surface of\r
the earth in which the temperature has been below freezing continuously from a\r
few to several thousands of years. Permafrost exists where the summer heating\r
fails to descend to the base of the layer of frozen ground. A continuous\r
stratum of permafrost is found where the\r
annual mean temperature is below about 5C (23F). <p>2. As limited in\r
application by P. F. Svetsov, soil that is known to have been frozen for at\r
least a century. <p>\r
Muller, S. W., 1947: Permafrost, or Permanently Frozen Ground, and\r
Related Engineering Problems, <p>\r
Hare, F. K., 1951: Compendium of Meteorology, p. 958, and map, p.\r
956."""@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/sciencekeywords> ;
    skos:narrower <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/d8606e80-3d34-4540-a355-5f99737f7ab7> ;
    skos:prefLabel "PERMAFROST"@en ;
    gcmd:reference [ gcmd:text """American Meteorological Society Glossary of Meteorology Online:\r
"http://amsglossary.allenpress.com/glossary\""""@en ] .

