<https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/1a5dc311-b702-4712-868a-f306bbdc0833> a skos:Concept ;
    skos:broader <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/8e8b7689-0a8e-47a4-9c68-5f6a207104d5> ;
    skos:changeNote """2021-12-01 12:46:23.0 [tstevens]  
update PrefLabel (Orbiting Solar Observatory (OSO)); 
""",
        """2022-03-07 12:27:00.0 [tstevens]  
insert Definition (id: null
text: The Orbiting Solar Observatory (abbreviated OSO) Program was the name of a series of American space telescopes primarily intended to study the Sun, though they also included important non-solar experiments. Eight were launched successfully into low Earth orbit by NASA between 1962 and 1975 using Delta rockets. Their primary mission was to observe an 11-year sun spot cycle in UV and X-ray spectra. The initial seven (OSO 1–7) were built by Ball Aerospace, then known as Ball Brothers Research Corporation (BBRC), in Boulder, Colorado. OSO 8 was built by Hughes Space and Communications Company, in Culver City, California.
language code: en); 
""" ;
    skos:definition "The Orbiting Solar Observatory (abbreviated OSO) Program was the name of a series of American space telescopes primarily intended to study the Sun, though they also included important non-solar experiments. Eight were launched successfully into low Earth orbit by NASA between 1962 and 1975 using Delta rockets. Their primary mission was to observe an 11-year sun spot cycle in UV and X-ray spectra. The initial seven (OSO 1–7) were built by Ball Aerospace, then known as Ball Brothers Research Corporation (BBRC), in Boulder, Colorado. OSO 8 was built by Hughes Space and Communications Company, in Culver City, California."@en ;
    skos:inScheme <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concepts/concept_scheme/platforms> ;
    skos:narrower <https://gcmd.earthdata.nasa.gov/kms/concept/3aea48c3-0abb-4c1a-87f7-4035473d0015>,
